<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sahnun</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">776–854</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as Sahnun ibn Said</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sahnun ibn Said was a jurist of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> and the most important early teacher of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. His Mudawwana, a large compilation of legal opinions from the circle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ibn-anas">Malik ibn Anas</a>, became a foundation text of the Maliki school, which came to dominate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. He served as chief judge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghlabid-dynasty">Aghlabids</a> from 848.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>His full name was Abu Said Abd al-Salam ibn Said ibn Habib al-Tanukhi; the nickname Sahnun, the name of a sharp bird, was given for the quickness of his mind. He came of an Arab family of the tribe of Tanukh that originated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, and his father was a soldier from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a>. In his youth he studied under the scholars of Qayrawan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, above all the Tripolitanian scholar Ali bin Ziyad, who had learned from Imam Malik himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 178 AH Sahnun travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> to study under other pupils of Malik, who had died before Sahnun could afford to reach him. He went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> to study with prominent scholars and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> in 191 AH. On accepting appointment as a judge he is said to have told his daughter Khadija that her father had that day been slain without a knife. He was known as scrupulous and courteous to litigants, but strict with the men around the emir: he would not let them send representatives to court, and he refused the emir's request not to interfere in their illegal ventures.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Sahnun was known for a strict orthodoxy, to the point of refusing to pray behind a Mu'tazilite imam. Until his time scholars of every tendency had spoken freely in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-mosque-of-kairouan">Great Mosque of Kairouan</a>; the Encyclopedia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> describes how he purged its circles, dispersing the Ibadi, Mu'tazilites and other sects, parading their leaders in disgrace and forcing some to recant in public. It counts him among the chief architects of the exclusive supremacy of Sunnism in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> form throughout the Muslim West. His son Muhammad ibn Sahnun, who died in 256 AH (870), was also a noted jurist.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sahnun” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.68,10.1&amp;year=826" aria-label="Kairouan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sahnun"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5698 4484h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5698 4484h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.68,10.1&amp;year=826">What was here in 826 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
